/-!
# The complete class for the two-sided sub-Gaussian null

The two-sided `1`-sub-Gaussian null on `ℝ`, `ProbabilityTheory.subgaussianNull 1`, is the set of
probability measures `μ` with `∫ exp (l x - l ^ 2 / 2) ∂μ ≤ 1` for every real `l`, that is, with
`HasSubgaussianMGF id 1 μ`. It is the null generated by the infinitely many constraints
`exp (l x - l ^ 2 / 2) - 1`. The
probability mixtures of the exponentials `exp (l x - l ^ 2 / 2)` are e-variables for it, but they
do not dominate every e-variable: `x ↦ x ^ 2` is one, and a mixture above it would have mean `1`
under the standard Gaussian law, hence be equal to `x ^ 2`, which vanishes at `0`.

The complete class is a family of Lévy–Khintchine type. The compensated kernel
`k (l, x) = (1 + l ^ 2) / l ^ 2 * (exp (l x - l ^ 2 / 2) - 1 - l x / (1 + l ^ 2))` at a nonzero
real `l`, with `k (0, x) = (x ^ 2 - 1) / 2` and `k (±∞, x) = -1`, is continuous in `l` on the
extended line `EReal`. Every nonnegative function `1 + a x + ∫ k (l, x) ∂ν(l)`, for a real `a` and
a finite measure `ν` on `EReal`, is an e-variable for the null, and every e-variable for the null
is dominated at every point by one of them. Mixtures `∫ exp (l x - l ^ 2 / 2) ∂π(l)` are the case
`ν(dl) = l ^ 2 / (1 + l ^ 2) π(dl)`, `a = ∫ l / (1 + l ^ 2) ∂π(l)`, and `x ^ 2` is the case
`a = 0`, `ν = 2 δ_0`. The domination holds for every function, measurable or not, whose mean is at
most `1` under the finitely supported members of the null.

This complete class is [EVR26, Note 21]. For the one-sided sub-Gaussian null, the probability
mixtures of the exponentials are the complete class [LRR26, Theorem 4.3]; for the two-sided null
they are not, and the class above replaces them.

## Main definitions

* `ProbabilityTheory.subgaussianCompensatedExp l x`: the compensated kernel `k (l, x)`.

## Main statements

* `ProbabilityTheory.integrable_subgaussianCompensatedExp_left`: each section `l ↦ k (l, x)` is
  integrable against a finite measure on `EReal`.
* `ProbabilityTheory.integrable_prod_subgaussianCompensatedExp`: under a `1`-sub-Gaussian
  probability law `μ` and a finite measure `ν`, the kernel is integrable on `μ.prod ν`.
* `ProbabilityTheory.isEVar_ofReal_one_add_integral_subgaussianCompensatedExp`: every nonnegative
  `1 + a x + ∫ k (l, x) ∂ν(l)` is an e-variable for the two-sided sub-Gaussian null.
* `ProbabilityTheory.exists_le_ofReal_one_add_integral_subgaussianCompensatedExp`: every function
  whose mean is at most `1` under the finitely supported members of the null is dominated at
  every point by a nonnegative `1 + a x + ∫ k (l, x) ∂ν(l)`.
* `ProbabilityTheory.IsEVar.exists_le_ofReal_one_add_integral_subgaussianCompensatedExp`: every
  e-variable for the null is dominated at every point by one of these functions.

## Implementation notes

The definitions and proofs are adapted from the Lean proof in
`EValuesResearch/Results/SubGaussianCompleteClass.lean` and
`EValuesResearch/Results/SubGaussianCompleteClassAux.lean` (commit `dd4072f`) of the paper project
`RemyDegenne/evalues_research` (same author), where the kernel is called `lkKernel`.

*Validity.* Under a member of the null, `x` has mean `0` and `x ^ 2` has mean at most `1`. At a
real `l`, `exp (l x - l ^ 2 / 2) - 1 = l ^ 2 / (1 + l ^ 2) * k (l, x) + l / (1 + l ^ 2) * x`, so
every section `k (l, ·)` has a nonpositive mean. Since `k ≥ -3 / 2`, Fubini's theorem applies.

*Completeness.* On a finite set `A` of points containing `0` and `±1`, the vectors
`(a y + ∫ k (l, y) ∂ν(l))_{y ∈ A}` and the vectors below them form a closed convex cone: the bound
`k (l, 0) ≤ -1 / 2` controls the mass of `ν`, the points `±1` control `a`, and the finite measures
of bounded mass on the compact space `EReal` form a compact set
(`isCompact_setOfPred_finiteMeasure_le_of_compactSpace`). If the vector of `E - 1` is outside
the cone, a separating functional is a nonnegative weighting of the points of `A` under which `x`
has mean `0` and every `k (l, ·)` a nonpositive mean: it is a finitely supported member of the
null under which `E` has mean above `1`. The finite intersection property in the compact set of
parameters with `ν` of mass at most `2` then gives one parameter for all points.

Every point `y` is charged by a finitely supported member of the null, the law of `y / n` times a
sum of `n ≥ y ^ 2` independent signs, so that the functions considered are finite.

## References

* [EVR26] *Answers to open questions in e-values and anytime-valid inference*, notes and the Lean
  library `EValuesResearch`, github.com/RemyDegenne/evalues_research, 2026.
* [LRR26] M. Larsson, A. Ramdas and J. Ruf, *Testing hypotheses generated by constraints*, Math.
  Oper. Res. (2026), arXiv:2504.02974v5.
-/

